\details{
The function calculates residual quantiles from the simulated data. For continuous distributions and method = "traditional", this will simply be the value of the ecdf.

\strong{Randomization procedure for discrete data}

For discrete data, there are two options implemented.

The current default (available since DHARMa 0.3.1) are probability integral transform (PIT-) residuals (Smith, 1985; Dunn & Smyth, 1996; see also Warton, et al., 2017). With k the number of simulations smaller than the observed value and t the number of simulations equal to it, the residual is (k + U (t + 1)) / (nSim + 1), with U ~ U(0,1), i.e. the rank of the observation among the nSim + 1 values, with ties broken at random, spread uniformly within its cell. Under H0, this rank is uniform on 0, ..., nSim (shown for continuous values by Talts et al., 2018), and spreading it within its cell, as in randomized rank histograms (Heinrich, 2021), makes the residual exactly uniform on (0, 1) for continuous, discrete and mixed distributions, also for small nSim. They are never exactly 0 or 1; observations outside the simulation envelope have residuals < 1/(nSim + 1) or > nSim/(nSim + 1) (see \link{testOutliers}).

Before DHARMa 0.3.1, a different randomization procedure was used, in which a U(-0.5, 0.5) distribution was added on observations and simulations for discrete distributions. For a completely discrete distribution, the two procedures should deliver equivalent results, but the second method has the disadvantage that (a) one has to know if the distribution is discrete (DHARMa tries to recognize this automatically), and (b) that it leads to inefficiencies for some distributions such as the Tweedie, which are partly continuous, partly discrete
(see e.g. \href{https://github.com/florianhartig/DHARMa/issues/168}{issue #168} on DHARMa GitHub page).

\strong{History of the residual calculation}
\itemize{
\item Up to DHARMa 0.3.0, residuals were calculated with the procedure that is now available as method = "traditional" (see above), with an empirical cumulative distribution function of the simulations that is 0 below and 1 above all simulations and interpolates linearly in between (since DHARMa 0.2.1).
\item DHARMa 0.3.1 introduced PIT residuals as default (\href{https://github.com/florianhartig/DHARMa/issues/168}{issue #168}), with ties randomized between k / nSim and (k + t) / nSim and, without ties, the interpolated empirical cumulative distribution function of the traditional method. Because this failed for constant simulations (\href{https://github.com/florianhartig/DHARMa/issues/195}{issue #195}), the interpolation was removed in DHARMa 0.3.3; from 0.3.3 to 0.5.1, residuals without ties were k / nSim, i.e. not randomized within the cells.
\item These k / nSim residuals lie on a grid of nSim + 1 values. Under H0, P(residual <= q) = (floor(q nSim) + 1) / (nSim + 1) instead of q (e.g. 0.51 instead of 0.5 for nSim = 50), so they are not uniform even for continuous responses. For 8000 observations simulated from the true model, \link{testUniformity} rejected in all simulated cases for nSim = 50 and in about 10\% for nSim = 250 (alpha = 0.05). Since DHARMa 0.5.2, PIT residuals are randomized within the cells as described above, which removes this bias (\href{https://github.com/florianhartig/DHARMa/issues/653}{issue #653}).
\item Since DHARMa 0.4.1, residuals with refit = TRUE are always calculated with method = "traditional", because the residuals simulated with refits are continuous also for discrete responses, so that PIT does not randomize discrete observations (\href{https://github.com/florianhartig/DHARMa/issues/272}{issue #272}). These residuals are not exactly uniform either: observations outside the simulation envelope get exactly 0 or 1 (with probability 1/(nSim + 1) each under H0). For 8000 observations and nSim = 50, \link{testUniformity} rejected in all simulated cases; for nSim = 250, in 3-4\%.
}

\strong{Rotation (optional)}

The getQuantile function includes an additional option to rotate residuals prior to calculating the quantile residuals. This option should ONLY be used when the fitted model includes a particular residual covariance structure, such as an AR1 or a spatial or phylogenetic CAR model.

For these models, residuals calculated from unconditional simulations will include the specified covariance structure, which will trigger e.g. temporal autocorrelation tests and can inflate type I errors of other tests. The idea of the rotation is to rotate the residual space according to the covariance structure of the fitted model, such that the rotated residuals are conditional independent (provided the fitted model is correct).

If the residual covariance of the fitted model at the response scale can be extracted (e.g. when fitting gls type models), it would be best to extract it and provide this covariance matrix to the rotation option. If that is not the case, providing the argument "estimated" to rotation will estimate the covariance from the data simulated by the model. This is probably without alternative for GLMMs, where the covariance at the response scale is likely not known / provided, but note that this approximation will tend to have considerable error and may be slow to compute for high-dimensional data. If you try to estimate the rotation from simulations, you should set n as high as possible! See \link{testTemporalAutocorrelation} for a practical example.

The rotation of residuals implemented here is similar to the Variogram.lme() and Variongram.gls() functions in nlme package using the argument resType = "normalized".
